# UniWorld Publishing Roadmap

Covers the full path from development-complete to public release. Structured in two
phases with a pause point for business development (incorporation, patent filing).

**Key principle**: Publish to GitHub (private) and fully validate before pausing.
Return after business milestones to go public, publish to registries, build website,
and promote. This ensures the codebase is release-ready and tested while business
preparation proceeds independently.

---

## Prerequisites (check before starting)

- [x] Rust core: all conformance tests passing (UAX #9, #14, #15, #29)
- [x] VS Code extension: 0.1.0 packaged (.vsix), icon, README, CHANGELOG, LICENSE
- [x] PowerShell module: Phases 1-3 done (all cmdlets working via Rust FFI)
- [x] PowerShell Phase 4: 68 Pester tests (all 12 cmdlets), comment-based help, README
- [ ] PowerShell Phase 4: Platform DLLs via CI (needs GitHub push to run)
- [x] GitHub Actions workflow: `.github/workflows/build-native.yml`
- [x] Publishing docs: CHECKLIST, MARKETING, OUTREACH_AND_LISTINGS, ACCOUNTS_AND_TOKENS, SITE_CONTENT

---

# PHASE A: Pre-Pause (Complete Before Business Development)

Everything below should be done in the current working session or shortly after.
Goal: fully tested, release-ready repo on GitHub (private), all accounts and tokens
acquired, nothing left that requires development work.

---

## Stage 1: Final Local Build and Full Test Suite

Run everything locally one final time to confirm the codebase is clean.

- [ ] **Rust core tests**: `cargo test` (all unit + conformance)
- [ ] **Rust core build (cffi)**: `cargo build --release --features cffi` (produces DLL)
- [ ] **VS Code extension compile**: `cd extensions/vscode; npm run compile`
- [ ] **VS Code extension package**: `cd extensions/vscode; npm run package` (produces .vsix)
- [ ] **VS Code extension test**: Install .vsix via F5 or `code --install-extension`, run through test guide
- [ ] **PowerShell Pester tests**: `Import-Module Pester -MinimumVersion 5.0; Invoke-Pester -Path extensions/powershell/Tests/ -Output Detailed` (all 68 pass)
- [ ] **PowerShell manual smoke test**: Import module, run each cmdlet category once
- [ ] **Version bumps**: Bump `UniWorld.psd1` to `0.1.0`; confirm `extensions/vscode/package.json` is `0.1.0`; confirm `Cargo.toml` version is aligned

---

## Stage 2: README Alignment and Cross-Linking

Ensure all READMEs are consistent, current, and link to each other before push.
**Status: Complete.** HAIMU AI development methodology, dev story, and cross-links in place; READMEs declared complete (edits reserved for developments before publishing).

**Scope of work** (estimated ~30 minutes with AI assistance):

- [x] **Root README** (`README.md`):
  - Verify it reflects the full project: library + VS Code + PowerShell + bindings
  - Add/verify links to: uniworld.world, GitHub repo, VS Code Marketplace (placeholder URL until published), PowerShell Gallery (placeholder), integration docs, Unicode Showcase
  - Add link to Grand Beta where appropriate
  - Add placeholder badge section (badges become live after registry publish)
- [x] **VS Code README** (`extensions/vscode/README.md`):
  - Verify links to uniworld.world, root README, PowerShell module
  - Verify features, settings table, and commands are current
- [x] **PowerShell README** (`extensions/powershell/README.md`):
  - Verify links to uniworld.world, root README, VS Code extension
  - Verify cmdlet table and examples are current
- [x] **Integration docs** (`docs/integration/`):
  - Verify each binding doc links to root README and relevant registry
- [x] **Cross-link audit**: Grep for broken or placeholder links; document any that depend on registry publication (these will be updated in Phase B)

---

## Stage 3: Signups and Procurement

Acquire all accounts, tokens, and the domain before pausing. This way, when you
return after business development, you can publish immediately without delays.

See `_publishing/ACCOUNTS_AND_TOKENS.md` for detailed steps for each.

- [ ] **GoDaddy: Purchase uniworld.world** (~$20-35 for first year)
  - Do NOT configure DNS yet (GitHub Pages isn't set up until Phase B)
  - Just purchase and hold the domain
- [ ] **Azure DevOps: Create org and PAT** for VS Code Marketplace publishing
  - Create publisher "aguywithai" via `vsce create-publisher`
  - Store PAT securely (password manager, not in repo)
- [ ] **PowerShell Gallery: Create account and API key**
  - Login at powershellgallery.com with Microsoft account
  - Generate API key scoped to "UniWorld"
  - Store key securely
- [ ] **crates.io: Login with GitHub** (one click, already have GitHub account)
  - Generate API token for `cargo publish`
- [ ] **PyPI: Create account** (optional; for when Python binding is published)
- [ ] **npm: Create account** (optional; for when WASM package is published)
- [ ] **Store all tokens** in password manager with notes on expiry dates

---

## Stage 4: GitHub Repository (Private)

- [ ] **Create repo**: `gh repo create aguywithai/uniworld --private --source=. --push`
  (or via github.com: New Repository -> uniworld -> Private)
- [ ] **Push code**: `git remote add origin https://github.com/aguywithai/uniworld.git; git push -u origin main`
- [ ] **Verify Actions**: Check Actions tab; `build-native.yml` triggers on push to main:
  - [ ] Windows build succeeds, uploads `uniworld.dll`
  - [ ] Linux build succeeds, uploads `libuniworld.so`
  - [ ] macOS build succeeds, uploads `libuniworld.dylib`
  - [ ] Pester (test-windows) job passes
  - [ ] Assemble job produces `uniworld-native-all` artifact
- [ ] **Fix any CI issues** and push fixes until all green

---

## Stage 5: Cross-Platform Testing with CI Artifacts

- [ ] **Download artifacts**: From Actions run, download `uniworld-native-all`
- [ ] **Place in module**: Copy to `extensions/powershell/native/win-x64/`, `linux-x64/`, `osx-arm64/`
- [ ] **WSL test**: Enter WSL, copy `libuniworld.so` to `native/linux-x64/`, run:
  ```bash
  pwsh -c "Import-Module ./extensions/powershell/UniWorld.psd1 -Force; Invoke-Pester -Path ./extensions/powershell/Tests/ -Output Detailed"
  ```
- [ ] **macOS test** (if collaborator available): Same Pester tests with `.dylib`
- [ ] **VS Code extension**: Final install of .vsix, full manual test pass
- [ ] **All tests green**: Confirm no regressions across platforms

---

## === PAUSE POINT: Business Development ===

At this point, the UniWorld codebase is:
- Fully tested locally (Rust, VS Code, PowerShell)
- On GitHub (private), CI passing on all 3 platforms
- Cross-platform artifacts built and verified
- All accounts created and tokens secured
- Domain purchased (DNS not yet configured)
- READMEs aligned and cross-linked

**Leave the repo here.** Proceed with:
- Business incorporation
- Provisional patent preparation and filing
- Other portfolio preparation

The repo will be ready to go public the moment you return. Nothing in the codebase
will change while you're away (unless you choose to make improvements).

**Recommended: live-publish testing before going fully public** (see Stage 6 notes).

---

# PHASE B: Post-Business Development (Return to Publish)

Resume here after incorporation and patent filing are secured.

---

## Stage 6: Go Public and Publish to Registries

**Pre-flight check**: Pull repo, run `cargo test`, run Pester tests, compile VS Code
extension -- confirm nothing has broken (Rust toolchain or dependency updates may
have occurred while away).

- [ ] **Pre-flight: local full test suite** (repeat Stage 1 tests)
- [ ] **Optional: live-publish testing**
  - VS Code: `vsce publish` then immediately set to **unlisted** in Marketplace dashboard
    (visible only via direct link for testing the install flow)
  - PowerShell: Publish pre-release version (`0.1.0-beta1`) to Gallery
  - Test: Install from Marketplace/Gallery on a clean machine or VM to verify
    the full install-and-use flow works outside your dev environment
  - Fix any issues found
- [ ] **Make repo public**: GitHub -> Settings -> Danger Zone -> Change visibility -> Public
- [ ] **Publish VS Code extension**: `cd extensions/vscode; vsce publish`
  - If previously published unlisted, update to listed
- [ ] **Publish PowerShell module**: `Publish-Module -Path . -NuGetApiKey $key`
  - If previously pre-release, publish stable `0.1.0`
- [ ] **Publish Rust crate**: `cargo publish` (from repo root)
- [ ] **Publish Python** (if ready): `maturin publish`
- [ ] **Publish npm** (if ready): `npm publish`
- [ ] **Tag release**: `git tag v0.1.0; git push origin v0.1.0`
- [ ] **Create GitHub Release**: Attach .vsix, native binaries, release notes
- [ ] **Badges**: Update root README with live badges (crates.io version, VS Code installs, license, etc.)

---

## Stage 7: Website Creation (GitHub Pages at uniworld.world)

- [ ] **Build site**: Follow `_publishing/MARKETING_AND_WEBSITE_DESIGN.md`
- [ ] **Content**: Hero, problem statement, feature grid, scripts covered, quick-start code, install cards, ecosystem links, Grand Beta footer
- [ ] **Deploy**: Enable GitHub Pages (Settings -> Pages -> main branch)
- [ ] **Configure DNS**: In GoDaddy, add A records and CNAME per `ACCOUNTS_AND_TOKENS.md`
- [ ] **Custom domain**: In GitHub repo Settings -> Pages -> Custom domain -> `uniworld.world`
- [ ] **Verify HTTPS**: Check "Enforce HTTPS" in Pages settings
- [ ] **Test**: Site loads at `https://uniworld.world` with all links working

---

## Stage 8: Screenshots and Final Polish

- [ ] **VS Code Marketplace screenshots**: Take screenshots of extension features in action (hover inspector, bidi visualization, grapheme cursor, status bar). Upload to Marketplace.
- [ ] **Update any placeholder links**: Now that all registries are live, replace any placeholder URLs in READMEs, integration docs, or website
- [ ] **Verify all cross-links**: Every README, doc page, and website link resolves correctly

